  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 206 PM EST MON NOV 16 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, highly amplified midlevel \nridging will be located over the eastern CONUS, with midlevel \ntroughing generally located over the western CONUS. A shortwave \ntrough will initially be centered over Wyoming, embedded within \nthis broader western CONUS flow; this feature will lift northeast \nthrough Wednesday and Thursday, following the periphery of this \nridge. Although this initial shortwave will not have any sensible \nimpacts on the forecast area, it will work to flatten the ridge \nsomewhat, with modest 500 mb height falls on Thursday. This will \nset the stage for the next system to traverse the CONUS this \nweekend. A deep trough initially located over the Intermountain \nWest will eject into the Plains, with some model indications that \nthis system will reach the Great Lakes region by Sunday. Although \nconsiderable uncertainty still exists regarding this system, this \nrepresents the best chance for rain of the forecast period. \nOtherwise, dry and uneventful weather is expected.\n\nAt the surface, high pressure will dominate the forecast through \nSaturday. Temperatures will warm a degree or two each day, \nremaining above average, with mid and upper level moisture also \nincreasing by Thursday as the midlevel ridge axis finally shifts \neast of the forecast area and flow aloft becomes more \nsouthwesterly. Even so, with such a weak pressure gradient in \nplace, surface winds will be light and variable, with no large \nscale moisture return expected at the surface. Thus, the biggest \nchange in the forecast at this point will be a transition from \n\"sunny\" to \"mostly sunny\" skies, with LREF mean PW still relegated\nto the 0.3 to 0.4 inch range by the end of the workweek, \nrepresenting an anomalously dry atmospheric column for this time \nof year. Of note, expected above average temperatures and \nrelatively low dewpoints will allow minimum RHs to reach near \ncritical to critical levels on Thursday, roughly in the 20-30% \nrange across the forecast area; however, winds will remain very \nlight, helping to mitigate fire weather concerns. With high \npressure remaining in the vicinity, a weak pressure gradient and \nlargely clear skies will promote the development of ridge/valley \nsplits each night, with ridgetop lows generally in the 40s while \nvalley temperatures fall into the low to mid 30s, again warming a \ndegree or two each night. \n\nModel spread increases significantly by the weekend as guidance\ndiffers regarding the progression of the aforementioned system.\nThe most recent NBM has introduced slight chance PoPs into the\nforecast area on Sunday, and with around 30% of LREF members\nshowing at least 0.1 inches of rain falling by the end of the\nperiod, this seems somewhat reasonable for now. However, with so\nmuch spatial uncertainty in the position of the associated surface\nlow, in addition to discrepancies regarding the quality of\nmoisture ahead of the system, confidence in any rainfall remains\nvery low at this time, and details will require refining in\nsubsequent forecasts.",
